# Task 5: Strategy & Leadership

## Q1: Sprint velocity is dropping due to flaky automation tests. How will you handle this?

    The first thing I would do is stop treating flaky tests as a test problem. 
    They are team problem. 
    When flaky tests keep failing for no real reason, engineers start ignoring failures, 
    and that's when actual bugs slip through. 
    So the priority is restoring trust in the suite before anything else.

    I would start by pulling the failure logs for the last two or three sprints and categorizing them
        1) environment issues, 
        2) timing/wait issues, 
        3) test data conflicts, 
        4) genuinely unstable application behavior. 

    In my experience, the majority of flakiness comes from one of two things 
        1) hardcoded waits 
        2) tests stepping on each other's data.

    Once we know the cause, we quarantine the flaky tests immediately. 
    They move out of the main pipeline into a separate run so they stop blocking the team. 
    This alone usually gives the velocity a bump because engineers aren't spending half their morning investigating failures that turn out to be nothing.
    From there we fix them in priority order. 
    Any test that's been flaky for more than two sprints and hasn't been fixed gets deleted, 
    a missing test is better than a lying one. I would also introduce a simple rule going forward 
    if a test fails three times in a row with no code changes, it gets flagged for review, not re-run indefinitely.
    On the team side, I will set aside time in each sprint specifically for test maintenance. 
    Automation debt is real, and if you don't budget time for it, it compounds.


## Q2: Two critical bugs were missed in QA and reported by the client in production. What's your approach?

The first step is to not be defensive about it. 
Critical bugs reaching production is a process failure, not a people failure, and treating it that way makes it easier to fix honestly.

Right after the incident, I'd do a quick RCA with the team — not a blame session, just a factual walkthrough. 
Where were these bugs? Were they in a feature we tested? Were they edge cases we didn't consider? 
Were they regression issues from an older area we assumed was stable? The answer changes what we fix.

If we tested the area but missed the bug, the question is whether our test cases were shallow. 
This usually means we were testing happy paths and not spending enough time on boundary conditions or integration points. I will review the test cases for that feature and fill the gaps.

If we didn't test the area at all, then we have a coverage problem. 
That could be scope creep during the sprint, unclear requirements, or assumptions about what the dev team already covered. 
In that case I will set up a proper handoff checklist between dev and QA so nothing falls in the gap.

Going forward I will also push for adding a regression test for every production bug we find. 
It sounds obvious but it often doesn't happen because teams are already moving to the next sprint. 
That one habit alone significantly reduces repeat escapes.

Finally, I will have a transparent conversation with the client. 
Not just an apology, but a brief explanation of what we found, what we changed, and how we're tracking it. 
Clients generally respond better to that than silence followed by a promise it won't happen again.
The above is a real time experience that I faced in 2024. Just FYI. 